{"verdict":"unsound","summary":"The protocol does not yet settle OUT-11: it omits the multistate mechanism and separate one-step estimator, and its supposed aggregate study is analyzed as individual-level survival data. More seriously, the STC implementation ignores Gauss-Hermite weights and all frequentist rows use a marginal log-cumulative-hazard transport rule that is false under the registered DGM, manufacturing part of the reported bias. The primary decision metric removes estimator-specific variance and was selected after observing the desired flexible-versus-PH separation. Several round-two defects are genuinely fixed, including the target-superpopulation definition, in-window crossings, and the within-row PH/flexible construction, but the study remains unsafe to run.","findings":[{"axis":"answers-problem","severity":"fatal","what":"OUT-11 asks for general-likelihood ML-NMR and a separate one-step exact-likelihood estimator against PH MAIC/STC across Weibull, Gompertz, and multistate mechanisms, plus the missing component-PAIC functionality. Version three explicitly omits the one-step estimator, multistate mechanisms, and implementation component. It can be a partial sub-study, but it cannot settle the stated catalog problem.","fix":"Either add the omitted estimator, mechanism, and implementation evaluation, or register a narrower question and state explicitly that OUT-11 remains open afterward."},{"axis":"answers-problem","severity":"fatal","what":"The alleged aggregate study is analyzed as IPD. The code fits flexsurvspline directly to d$agd individual event and censoring records and bootstraps individuals from that trial. This evaluates a two-IPD-study analysis, not an indirect comparison using published aggregate survival information, reconstructed Kaplan-Meier data, or a general-likelihood aggregate-data likelihood.","fix":"Define exactly what is observable from the aggregate trial, generate that representation, and restrict every estimator to it. If pseudo-IPD reconstruction is intended, implement the reconstruction identically and label the evidence accordingly."},{"axis":"answers-problem","severity":"serious","what":"E2 is inconsistent with the network. The target study contains PBO and B, so an unadjusted Cox fit there cannot estimate the transported B-versus-A contrast whose crossing times are tabulated. The reported crossing times correspond algebraically to beta_B minus beta_A, suggesting PH-test power was calibrated in a hypothetical direct A-versus-B trial rather than either observed source comparison.","fix":"Specify the E2 arms and data explicitly. Calibrate PH-test power for the actually observed A-versus-PBO and B-versus-PBO trials, or label a simulated direct A-versus-B experiment as a separate diagnostic."},{"axis":"answers-problem","severity":"serious","what":"The protocol says the title identifies a partial Weibull/Gompertz benchmark, but the actual title does not mention partial scope, either family, the missing multistate mechanism, or the omitted one-step estimator.","fix":"Change the title and catalog-status claim to match the actual scope."},{"axis":"dgm-builds-in-finding","severity":"fatal","what":"E1 and E2 are still not numerically locked. Section 5 reports gamma varying to 0.50 and a zero-variance ablation, while the locked configuration contains only gamma 0.30 and the gamma-zero control. E2 does not identify its family, beta_B solution, gamma value, or exact treatment contrast.","fix":"Add executable E1 and E2 cell builders containing every gamma level, variance level, family, treatment contrast, beta value, censoring regime, and replicate count."},{"axis":"dgm-builds-in-finding","severity":"serious","what":"The qualitative flexible-versus-PH result is largely imposed by kappa_B equal to 0.15 or 0.30 while kappa_A is zero, coupled with flexible models that add exactly the treatment-by-time structure denied to the PH rows. Solved beta_B values such as -0.2706 and -0.1431 then force crossings and decision proximity. This is legitimate as a stress test, but it cannot establish general estimator superiority.","fix":"Include generators not nested in any fitted flexible basis, varied crossing shapes and locations, and scenarios where extra flexibility costs efficiency. Limit conclusions to the registered DGM classes."},{"axis":"estimand","severity":"fatal","what":"STC does not integrate over N(0.60,1). STC_NODES stores Gauss-Hermite weights, but rp_logH averages survival equally across nodes with mean and never uses STC_NODES$w. Equal weighting of Gauss-Hermite nodes represents a different, highly distorted target distribution.","fix":"Compute the weighted sum of conditional survival at each time using the normalized quadrature weights, verify it against high-order quadrature and Monte Carlo integration, then discard and rerun the pilot because its STC biases and D1 calibration are invalid."},{"axis":"estimand","severity":"fatal","what":"The shared transport rule does not target the registered A curve when gamma is nonzero and study baselines differ. Under kappa_A=0, the truth is E[exp{-H_0,tgt(t) exp(beta_A+gamma X)}]. The code instead estimates a marginal log-cumulative-hazard ratio at H_0,ipd(t) and multiplies H_0,tgt(t) by that ratio. Because marginalization is nonlinear, that ratio depends on the baseline cumulative hazard and is not transportable. The registered gamma=0.30 and differing Weibull scales 12 versus 14 or Gompertz levels 1/25 versus 1/30 activate the error by construction.","fix":"For STC, transport the conditional beta and gamma relationship to the target placebo baseline and only then marginalize over target X. For MAIC, acknowledge that a marginal IPD-study curve ratio does not identify target RMST across a different baseline without an additional structural model."},{"axis":"estimand","severity":"serious","what":"The promised pointwise interval coverage at 6, 12, and 18 months is not implemented in the supplied frequentist bootstrap. freq_boot stores and returns only rmst_diff; all surv_diff bootstrap values and intervals are discarded.","fix":"Bootstrap every registered survival-difference outcome and return its pointwise intervals before claiming that time-indexed calibration is implemented."},{"axis":"fair-comparison","severity":"fatal","what":"ML-NMR is allowed to transport through a conditional structural model, while the frequentist rows are forced through the invalid marginal log-cumulative-hazard graft. Thus the methods do not receive different implementations of the same valid estimand map; STC and MAIC are structurally handicapped before sampling begins.","fix":"Give every method the strongest valid transport operation implied by that method, distinguish estimators that cannot identify target RMST, and do not force all methods through a mathematically false common rule merely to make outputs comparable."},{"axis":"fair-comparison","severity":"serious","what":"Frequentist failures are handled more favorably than Bayesian failures. freq_boot silently removes failed resamples and forms percentile intervals from the survivors, while nonconverged MAIC weights are used despite the returned convergence flag. Stan failures, by contrast, trigger refitting and failure reporting.","fix":"Register a minimum successful-bootstrap fraction, treat failed resamples and failed weighting fits explicitly, and apply comparable failure accounting across methods."},{"axis":"fair-comparison","severity":"serious","what":"The cited original MAIC and STC papers do not establish that these custom flexible Royston-Parmar implementations, marginalized quadrature STC, or the shared log-cumulative-hazard transport rule are the specifications recommended by their authors. CrossRef verifies metadata, not methodological fidelity.","fix":"Cite methodological sources that actually define each implemented variant and transport operation, or label the rows as protocol-specific extensions rather than canonical MAIC/STC."},{"axis":"decision-rule","severity":"fatal","what":"D1 is not recommendation performance. Subtracting a hypothetical error floor computed from each estimator's own variance deliberately removes efficiency, even though efficiency is a method property. As sigma tends to infinity, both the raw error rate and the proposed floor tend to 0.5, so an arbitrarily noisy estimator can have zero excess and pass.","fix":"Use raw expected recommendation loss or another utility-based risk that retains both bias and variance. If a variance-normalized statistic is retained, label it as a bias diagnostic rather than fit-for-purpose performance."},{"axis":"decision-rule","severity":"fatal","what":"The 0.10 cutoff was chosen because a 60-replicate pilot on the same cells separated the flexible rows from the PH rows. New random replicates from the same tuned cells do not provide independent confirmation. The claimed protection from not piloting ML-NMR is also weak because section 7 reports observed flexible and PH ML-NMR results from a check replicate.","fix":"Choose the tolerance from an external decision or error criterion, or reserve genuinely held-out DGM families and parameter regions for confirmation. Treat the current D1 comparison as exploratory."},{"axis":"decision-rule","severity":"serious","what":"The supposed noise floor assumes a normal sampling distribution and estimates sigma from only 40 replicates per cell. A recommendation-error proportion near 0.37 has per-cell Monte Carlo standard error about 0.076, and taking absolute cell excess creates a positive noise floor even for an unbiased method. The Monte Carlo interval construction is not specified, while the pass rule mixes a 0.10 point threshold with an unrelated 0.20 interval threshold.","fix":"Define the interval algorithm and complete classification rule, assess the null distribution of mean absolute excess, and increase replicates until the 0.10 distinction is resolvable."},{"axis":"decision-rule","severity":"fatal","what":"The integration-order decision cannot resolve its own threshold. The protocol estimates a paired-difference standard error of 0.024 months but selects an order when its difference from 256 points is below 0.02. It uses a noisy point comparison, and 256 points is itself acknowledged not to be a converged reference.","fix":"Increase posterior draws and calibration replicates, compare against higher orders or an independently validated reference, and require an uncertainty bound rather than a point difference to fall below 0.02."},{"axis":"decision-rule","severity":"serious","what":"Pooled coverage can look nominal by averaging undercoverage in difficult crossing cells with overcoverage in controls. With only 40 replicates per cell, the protocol has made cell-specific calibration descriptive and therefore cannot support a general statement that an estimator is calibrated over the scenario set.","fix":"Increase per-cell replication or define the target explicitly as deployment-average coverage and avoid claims about scenario-level calibration."},{"axis":"feasibility","severity":"fatal","what":"The executable configuration still has N_INT=NA, and section 14 permits cutting N_REP if 256 points are needed without specifying the resulting replicate count or minimum precision. The final design is therefore not yet frozen or executable.","fix":"Complete a statistically adequate integration study, hard-code N_INT and the final N_REP, and rerun the Monte Carlo-resolution calculations before any reported replicate."},{"axis":"feasibility","severity":"serious","what":"The Stan runtime is understated by about a factor of two after changing from two to four chains. A four-chain fit may take the same elapsed time as a two-chain fit when run alone, but it consumes twice the cores and halves throughput across 960 fits on an eight-core machine. Under the protocol's own timings, the 64-point Stan work is about 9.9 hours rather than 4.9, and the 128-point work about 22.3 rather than 11.1, before bootstrap and refits.","fix":"Recalculate throughput using four cores per fit and include doubled-iteration refits, calibration, and all sensitivity arms."},{"axis":"feasibility","severity":"serious","what":"The reported at-risk fractions omit censoring and cannot apply to both balanced and differential cells. For example, a target B survival of 0.239 at month 18 with censoring rate 0.100 gives an observed at-risk fraction of 0.239 times exp(-1.8), about 0.0395, or roughly eight of 200 participants, not 0.239. The single PH-test power value per row likewise cannot describe both censoring regimes.","fix":"Tabulate S(t)G(t), event fractions, and PH-test power separately for every actual study arm and censoring cell, then reassess whether month 18 is supported."},{"axis":"feasibility","severity":"serious","what":"The runtime omits the unspecified 2- and 5-knot complexity subset, the prior-sensitivity subset, the ongoing integration sweep, and the real cost of the stated 10% doubled-iteration refits. The subsets are called prespecified but are not identified.","fix":"Name every sensitivity cell and replicate, include it in the fit count, and add a measured contingency for failures."},{"axis":"citation","severity":"serious","what":"Struthers and Kalbfleisch and Xu and O'Quigley support a censoring- and event-weighted least-false Cox projection, but they do not imply that non-movement is mathematically impossible for every non-proportional contrast and every pair of censoring regimes. Special weighting cancellations can produce equal projections.","fix":"Replace the impossibility claim with a qualified statement and let the registered numerical ablations establish movement for this DGM."},{"axis":"citation","severity":"serious","what":"Signorovitch et al. supports MAIC broadly but not the claim that matching a centered second moment is its standard authored specification. Ishak et al. does not validate the particular marginalized Royston-Parmar STC and marginal transport rule used here, and Bucher et al. does not validate transporting a baseline-dependent marginal log-cumulative-hazard contrast.","fix":"Use citations specific to the implemented extensions and separate bibliographic attribution from evidence that an implementation follows author recommendations."},{"axis":"citation","severity":"minor","what":"IDN-05 remains an internal label without a link, bibliographic record, or reproducible artifact, despite the claimed resolution saying it is named and linked. Its 8.3% result therefore cannot be checked from this protocol.","fix":"Provide a stable identifier and exact report or artifact location, or remove its numerical result as external justification."}],"round2_resolution":[{"finding":"Censoring absent from matrix, replicate count, and runtime","resolved":"partly","note":"E3 cell counting is corrected, but runtime is wrong under four chains and E1/E2 remain incompletely enumerated."},{"finding":"DGM not numerically locked","resolved":"partly","note":"Most E3 parameters are fixed, but E1 gamma and variance levels, E2 settings, N_INT, and the possible replacement N_REP remain unset."},{"finding":"Decision threshold confused with bias tolerance","resolved":"partly","note":"Truths now straddle 0.50 and mean absolute cell bias removes signed cancellation, but the replacement D1 metric is invalid and pilot-tuned."},{"finding":"MAIC and STC cannot consume three aggregate studies","resolved":"yes","note":"The network now has one IPD and one target study, although the new implementation treats the target study as IPD rather than aggregate data."},{"finding":"PH and flexible columns changed likelihood and basis","resolved":"yes","note":"Within each RP row the likelihood and basis are held fixed and treatment-by-time terms are toggled; Cox is separated."},{"finding":"Equal knot counts asserted equal flexibility","resolved":"partly","note":"The equivalence claim is withdrawn, but the sensitivity subset is not actually enumerated."},{"finding":"Superpopulation truth versus realized target sample","resolved":"yes","note":"Known target mean and variance are supplied to all methods and the estimand is explicitly superpopulation marginal."},{"finding":"No time-indexed calibration outcome","resolved":"partly","note":"Times 6, 12, and 18 are registered, but the supplied bootstrap does not produce survival-difference intervals needed for coverage."},{"finding":"Coverage unresolvable with no inconclusive outcome","resolved":"yes","note":"The Monte Carlo arithmetic is corrected and an inconclusive category is defined for pooled coverage."},{"finding":"Four-regime PH plug-in statistic underspecified","resolved":"yes","note":"The fixed baseline, range statistic, uniform weights, and analytic least-false input are now stated."},{"finding":"Priors, sensitivity, bootstrap interval, and resampling unit absent","resolved":"partly","note":"Priors, percentile intervals, and within-arm individual resampling are specified, but sensitivity subsets and bootstrap-failure rules are not."},{"finding":"R-hat alone used as sampler policy","resolved":"yes","note":"Derived-estimand ESS, divergences, treedepth, refitting, and recorded failures are added."},{"finding":"Integration citation argued against chosen order","resolved":"no","note":"A selection rule replaces the citation, but its standard error exceeds its tolerance, 256 is not validated, and N_INT remains unset."},{"finding":"Runtime omitted censoring and bootstrap","resolved":"no","note":"Bootstrap timing is measured, but four-chain throughput is miscomputed and refits and sensitivity arms remain omitted."},{"finding":"Two censoring pathways called exactly separable","resolved":"yes","note":"The additive claim is withdrawn and the analysis is framed as a factorial with interaction and exact ablations only."},{"finding":"Study 5 unidentified, novelty unsupported, one-step equivalence unproved","resolved":"partly","note":"The equivalence claim is withdrawn and novelty is narrowed, but IDN-05 is still not linked or reproducibly identified."},{"finding":"Broad title overstates partial benchmark","resolved":"no","note":"The text calls the study partial, but the actual title remains broad and does not name Weibull, Gompertz, or the omissions."},{"finding":"No in-window crossing","resolved":"yes","note":"The registered beta_B values produce conditional and marginal crossings inside the supported time window algebraically."},{"finding":"Bootstrap called cheap without measurement","resolved":"partly","note":"The 0.658-second measurement and 43.9 core-hour arithmetic are supplied, but the total runtime remains understated."},{"finding":"Comparator authorship uncited","resolved":"yes","note":"The named methods now have bibliographically plausible author and DOI attributions."},{"finding":"Marginal-hazard-ratio novelty overclaimed","resolved":"yes","note":"The established marginal mechanism is credited to Hernan, Aalen-Cook-Roysland, and Stensrud-Hernan, and the novelty claim is narrowed."},{"finding":"Gamma role ambiguous","resolved":"yes","note":"Gamma is explicitly shared by A and B and cancels from their conditional contrast."},{"finding":"MAIC exact normal transport unremarked","resolved":"partly","note":"The exact exponential-tilt property is now mentioned, but matching the second moment does not remove the idealized exact-normal advantage granted by the DGM."},{"finding":"D1 pooling ambiguous","resolved":"partly","note":"Uniform cell weights and mean absolute cell excess are specified, but the resulting statistic is not recommendation risk and its interval rule remains incomplete."}],"citation_problems":[{"cite":"Struthers and Kalbfleisch 1986; Xu and O'Quigley 2000","problem":"They support censoring-dependent least-false Cox projections, not the universal claim that non-movement under non-proportional hazards is asymptotically impossible."},{"cite":"Signorovitch et al. 2010","problem":"The citation supports MAIC, but not the claim that centered second-moment matching and the protocol's flexible survival implementation are the original authors' standard specification."},{"cite":"Ishak et al. 2015","problem":"The paper does not establish the protocol's quadrature-marginalized Royston-Parmar STC or its marginal log-cumulative-hazard transport operation as the authored STC method."},{"cite":"Bucher et al. 1997","problem":"Bucher supports anchored indirect comparison on a compatible transportable effect scale; it does not justify grafting a baseline-dependent marginal log-cumulative-hazard ratio onto a different baseline."},{"cite":"CrossRef verification","problem":"CrossRef can verify metadata and authorship, not whether a paper recommends the exact estimator, variance procedure, basis, or transport rule implemented."},{"cite":"IDN-05","problem":"It has no stable citation or link in the payload, so its reported 8.3% result and integration finding are not independently checkable."}],"what_is_sound":["The protocol correctly distinguishes conditional proportional-hazards coefficients from marginal and non-proportional hazard-ratio behavior.","The primary truth is defined on the target-superpopulation marginal scale at a fixed 18-month horizon, and the marginal hazard is correctly defined as E[hS]/E[S].","Supplying known target moments resolves the prior realized-target-sample versus superpopulation mismatch.","The solved beta_B values place truths on both sides of the decision boundary, and the reported B-versus-A crossing times are algebraically consistent with those values.","The gamma=0, kappa_B=0 control is an appropriate exact marginal-PH negative control.","Within each Royston-Parmar row, the PH and flexible variants now use the same likelihood and basis, with Cox kept outside that factorial contrast.","The least-false Cox score equation and the decision to size rather than rediscover the known censoring-weighting phenomenon are reasonable.","The author and DOI attributions for Phillippo, Bucher, Royston-Parmar, Grambsch-Therneau, Hernan, Aalen-Cook-Roysland, and Stensrud-Hernan appear bibliographically correct."]}
